% ============================================================================
% vocabulary-in-context — words whose meaning a primary source teaches via
% a worked context-clue example sentence (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% The TENTH literacy sub-skill library in this loop's sweep. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? vocabulary_in_context(ornithology, $Meaning)
%       % scientific_study_of_birds
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `vocabulary_in_context(word, meaning)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the meaning WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any word the source does not define this way.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a meaning and recall which word it
% belongs to:
%
%     ? vocabulary_in_context($W, hidden_or_not_easily_seen)
%       % inconspicuous
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why `meaning` is a short constant-style label rather than
% a full-sentence definition: `fable-moral.adj` (shipped earlier in this
% loop) discovered that the ADJ query grammar accepts a quoted-string
% literal as a `table` row VALUE but NOT as a query ARGUMENT — a query can
% only ground an atom/number or bind a $Var. Using a short atom label here
% (rather than a full-sentence meaning, as `fable-moral.adj`'s `moral`
% column does) keeps BOTH the direct query (`vocabulary_in_context(word,
% $Meaning)`) and the reverse query (`vocabulary_in_context($W,
% meaning_label)`) usable as ordinary ground-argument binding queries,
% without hitting that limitation.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Using Context Clues
% to Understand Word Meanings" article, which gives three worked
% context-clue examples — a target word, an example sentence containing
% it, and the word's meaning as revealed by that sentence's own context:
%
%     word            example sentence (verbatim)                                                                          meaning (this table's label)
%     -------------   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------   -----------------------------------
%     ornithology     "People who study birds are experts in ornithology."                                                 scientific_study_of_birds
%     frugivorous     "Frugivorous birds prefer eating fruit to any other kind of food."                                   eats_fruit_as_primary_food
%     inconspicuous   "Some birds like to build their nests in inconspicuous spots — high up in the tops of trees,         hidden_or_not_easily_seen
%                      well hidden by leaves."
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust consensus` — the same tier as
% the other Reading Rockets citations already shipped in this directory.
% A word that is NOT one of these three — e.g. `arboreal`, a real
% vocabulary word but not defined by this specific cited page — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a meaning the source never gave. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table vocabulary_in_context {
    columns word, meaning

    row (ornithology, scientific_study_of_birds)
    row (frugivorous, eats_fruit_as_primary_food)
    row (inconspicuous, hidden_or_not_easily_seen)

    source "People who study birds are experts in ornithology."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/topics/vocabulary/articles/using-context-clues-understand-word-meanings"
    trust consensus
}
